  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 345 AM EDT WED SEP 30 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Thursday evening with a weak surface ridge \nover the Southeast CONUS with a ~1020 mb surface high over the \nSoutheast beneath a strong upper-level high centered over the \nSoutheast coastline. Further upstream, an upper-level closed low \nwill be rapidly dampening into an upper trough over the Central \nPlains while another upper low develops in vicinity of the Western \nTX Panhandle Friday. Recent LREF runs have trended noticeably more \namplified with this pattern, deepening the southwestern low while \nstrengthening the ridge over the Eastern US. This enhanced \ndownstream ridge and strong southerly warm-air advection (WAA) \nacross the Ohio Valley account for the warming pre-frontal \ntemperatures in recent guidance, supporting highs across Eastern \nKentucky in the mid to upper 80s on Thursday and upper 70s to mid \n80s on Friday.\n\nAn initial cold front will sink eastward toward the Commonwealth on \nFriday, drawing rich Gulf moisture up the Mississippi and Ohio \nValleys and bringing an increasing coverage of showers and a few \nembedded thunderstorms. As the southern-stream upper trough opens up \nand the surface boundary stalls near the Ohio River/Appalachians, \nshower and thunderstorm chances will linger across Eastern Kentucky \nthrough Saturday. Temperatures will cool into the mid 60s to mid 70s \non Saturday under persistent cloud cover and intermittent rain.\n\nA secondary northern-stream trough will dig into the Great Lakes and \nOhio Valley Sunday morning, pushing a reinforcing cold front through \nthe region and steadily lowering rain chances from northwest to \nsoutheast through Sunday afternoon and evening (highs in the lower \n70s). However, as the main trough axis slowly sweeps across the \nAppalachians, isolated shower chances could remain along the KY-TN \nand KY-VA borders through Sunday evening. Drier continental high \npressure takes full control by Monday morning, shutting off any \nremaining precipitation. While daytime temperatures remain pleasant \nin the mid 60s to low 70s Monday and Tuesday, clear skies and light \nwinds Monday night will allow cooler lows in the 40s to return to \nthe area.",
